<p>The frames are HTML, and that is the right source of truth: change a word in the page and every export is right. But a thumbnail is often finished by hand — a tile nudged, a word retyped, a picture swapped. So the frames can be <strong>rebuilt as layered Pixelmator documents</strong>: the picture without its words on one layer, and every piece of text as a real text layer in the real font, at its real size and position, in the system’s colours. Open one, retype the title, move the note, export.</p>

<h2>Two commands</h2>
{{{{code "bash"}}}}
npm run build
npm run frames -- --layers     # exports/frames/<name>.png, .ground.png, .json — through your own Chrome
npm run pixelmator             # exports/pixelmator/<name>.pxd — through Pixelmator Pro itself
npm run pixelmator thumb-film channel-art    # just those
{{{{/code}}}}
<p><code>frames</code> opens each page under <code>/frames/</code> at its size, finishes every animation, and writes the picture, the picture with the words made transparent (the <em>ground</em>), and a manifest of every text run — what it says, where it sits, how big, which face, which weight, what colour, how far it is tilted. <code>pixelmator</code> reads the manifest, writes one AppleScript per frame, and has Pixelmator Pro build the document: the ground as an image layer, a text layer per run, grouped as <em>Words</em>, saved as <code>.pxd</code>. Nothing is rasterised twice; the fonts are the ones installed from <code>assets/fonts/</code> (install them once from there, or from the <code>geist</code> package and Google Fonts, so Pixelmator can set them).</p>

<h2>What the layers are</h2>
<div class="im-table-scroll"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it is</th><th>Edit it for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><b>Ground</b></td><td>The frame with every word transparent: the paper, the pattern, the photograph, the tiles, the trail, the chips’ fills, the dot.</td><td>Swapping the picture: replace the layer, or mask a new photograph under it.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Words / …</b></td><td>One text layer per run of text, named by its first words. Geist for titles, Geist Mono for facts and chips, Caveat for notes, Geist Pixel for numerals.</td><td>Retyping anything. Moving a note. Changing a tile’s word.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2>Honest limits</h2>
<ul>
	<li>Text that wraps in the browser becomes one layer per line, because that is how it was measured; join two if you retype a title.</li>
	<li>Letter-spacing, caps and tilt are carried; a text-shadow or a stroke is not — it is on the ground under the transparent word, so the hole still reads.</li>
	<li>Pixelmator needs the fonts installed by their PostScript names (<code>Geist-SemiBold</code>, <code>GeistMono-Medium</code>, <code>Caveat-Regular</code>, <code>GeistPixel-Square</code>). The script says which it could not set.</li>
	<li>macOS only, Pixelmator Pro only; the export step runs anywhere Chrome does.</li>
</ul>
